alsa: take the time64 timespec fix, cpal and rodio still from git

On Debian trixie, 32-bit libasound writes a 16-byte `struct timespec`.
`libc::timespec` stays 8 bytes on arm-unknown-linux-gnueabihf, so
`Status::get_htstamp` writes 8 bytes past the slot its caller reserved,
overwrites the saved lr and returns into address 0. cpal asks for that
timestamp on every callback, so it takes the first note played.

The fix spans three crates, two of them released on 2026-07-31:

  alsa-sys 0.6.1       diwic/alsa-sys#20 — compiles a probe measuring
                       sizeof(snd_htimestamp_t) against the ALSA headers and,
                       when it is 16, defines alsa-sys' own 16-byte timespec
                       instead of re-exporting libc's.
  alsa 0.12.1          diwic/alsa-rs#158 — `alsa::timespec` (alsa-sys')
                       replaces libc::timespec in pcm, rawmidi and ump.
  RustAudio/cpal#1285  still open — bumps alsa to 0.12 and alsa-sys to 0.6,
                       and carries timestamps as `alsa::timespec`.

So the two alsa crates are plain version requirements here, and only cpal
needs a git pin. The requirements name the patch releases: alsa 0.12.0 still
takes its timespec from libc, and alsa-sys 0.6.0 still re-exports it.

rodio is pinned only because cpal 0.18 needs it: released rodio 0.22.2 asks
for cpal ^0.17, and `links = "alsa"` forbids two alsa-sys copies in one
graph, so the chain has to move as a whole. rodio master still declares
version 0.22.2, so qbz's `rodio = "0.22"` requirement is unchanged.

The alternative was RUST_LIBC_UNSTABLE_GNU_TIME_BITS=64, which widens
libc::timespec for the whole crate graph. Besides being unstable, it does not
build here: with a time64 libc, `timespec` gains a private __pad field, and
alsa 0.11 — what this workspace resolved — fills its timespec literals field
by field. Upstream fixed that in 0.12 by constructing with zeroed(), which is
another reason the bump is the way forward rather than the env var.

qbz names neither timespec nor htstamp, so this is a dependency change only.
Revs rather than branches, so a rebuild resolves the same sources.

The two releases are byte-identical to the revs this patch used to pin —
`src/` in both, plus alsa-sys' `build.rs` — so only where cargo fetches them
changed.

Verified with `cargo tree -p qbzd -e normal`: a single alsa-sys 0.6.1
(crates.io) in the graph, alsa 0.12.1 (crates.io), cpal 0.18.1 (git), rodio
0.22.2 (git). cpal 0.18 also brings its new PulseAudio backend into the graph
via rodio's default features; the `pulseaudio` crate is pure Rust (no build
script, no libpulse), so the NEEDED list is unchanged.

Drop this patch once a cpal release carries #1285 and rodio requires that
cpal; the alsa requirements then go upstream on their own.

